Optimal Solution - Greedy approach that minimizes jumps by expanding the farthest reachable range and jumping only when necessary.
                    I treat each jump as a range and greedily expand the farthest reachable index before committing to the next jump.

class Solution {
    public int jump(int[] nums) {
        // Total number of jumps needed
        int totaljumps = 0;
        // Destination index (last index)
        int dest = nums.length - 1;
        // coverage = farthest index we can reach so far
        int coverage = 0;
        // lastjumpIdx = end of the range for the current jump
        int lastjumpIdx = 0;
        // If array has only one element, no jump needed
        if (nums.length == 1)
            return 0;
        // Traverse the array
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            // Update the farthest reachable index
            coverage = Math.max(coverage, i + nums[i]);
            // If we've reached the end of the current jump range
            if (i == lastjumpIdx) {
                // Make a jump
                lastjumpIdx = coverage;
                totaljumps++;
                // If we can already reach or pass the destination
                if (coverage >= dest)
                    return totaljumps;
            }
        }
        return totaljumps;
    }
}

Time Complexity :- O(N).
Space Complexity :- O(1).
